  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 101 PM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nThe long term forecast will be quite repetitive with daily \nhighs in the mid 90s to around 100 through at least Saturday. \nDew points are likely to stay in the 60s (LREF probabilities for\nafternoon Td > 70 is generally 0-5%), which is a drier trend \nthat looks to continue through the weekend. The forecast dew \npoints and heat index values may be too high Friday and \nSaturday. This will bring down heat index values to near the dry\nbulb temperature. This makes it unlikely that the area will see\nheat index values above 105, and some areas may barely see 100.\nA Heat Advisory continues through Friday. There is potential it\nwill need to continue into Saturday, but LREF only has around \n50% probabilities for heat index above 100, so for now will not\nextend it.\n\nLooking longer term, ensemble data and CPC forecasts continue to\nindicate above normal temperatures and below normal\nprecipitation. Notably, ECMWF and GFS ensemble mean temperature\nanomalies around +10-15F continue through next week, as do\nnegative precipitation anomalies. Ensemble probabilities for\ntotal precipitation of < 0.1\" through this week are generally\n89-100% and through next week they show around 50% (but 70-80%\nchance of < 0.5\"). In other words, hot and dry will continue to\ndominate the forecast, resulting in increased drought and\nwildfire risks with time.",
